#: ../../source/references_updaters-and-trackers.md:3
msgid "The problem updaters solve"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_updaters-and-trackers.md:5
msgid ""
"Normal animations are discrete: `self.play()` goes from state A to state "
"B. But what if you need continuous relationships — a label that always "
"hovers above a moving dot, or a line that always connects two points?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_updaters-and-trackers.md:7
msgid ""
"Without updaters, you'd manually reposition every dependent object before"
" every `self.play()`. Five animations that move a dot means five manual "
"repositioning calls for the label. Miss one and it freezes in the wrong "
"spot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_updaters-and-trackers.md:9
msgid ""
"Updaters let you declare a relationship ONCE. Manim calls the updater "
"function EVERY FRAME (15-60 fps depending on quality) to enforce that "
"relationship, no matter what else is happening."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_updaters-and-trackers.md:11
msgid "ValueTracker: an invisible steering wheel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_updaters-and-trackers.md:13
msgid ""
"A ValueTracker is an invisible Mobject that holds a single float. It "
"never appears on screen. It exists so you can ANIMATE it while other "
"objects REACT to its value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_updaters-and-trackers.md:15
msgid ""
"Think of it as a slider: drag the slider from 0 to 5, and every object "
"wired to it responds in real time."
msgstr ""

